   "id": "677022db-5b11-4552-b88b-47a9d53ed9ed",
   "term": "Daisy Chain",
   "verdict": "NEEDS_REVISION",
   "suggestions": [
    {
     "field": "definition",
     "severity": "Medium",
     "issue": "The definition opens \"Wiring devices in series, looping from one to the next,\" but practical_application then says each 70V speaker transformer is connected \"in parallel as the cable passes by,\" and a listed common_mistake warns against \"Confusing parallel 70V tap connections with series low-impedance wiring.\" A beginner is told series, then parallel, then warned about confusing the two within one entry \u2014 a genuine learning trap about the term itself.",
     "suggestion": "Separate the cabling topology from the electrical connection: e.g. \"A daisy chain runs one cable device-to-device in a chained (looped) path rather than a home run to each device. Note the cabling path being chained does not mean the loads are wired electrically in series \u2014 on a 70V/100V line the speaker taps sit electrically in parallel across the shared pair.\" Drop the bare word \"series\" from the opening or qualify it as the cable path, not the electrical connection."
    }
   ]
